.TH ZIPDEDUP 1 "October 2026" "Xtro-ZIP" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zipdedup \- convert duplicate ZIP members into WinZip Method 92 references
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zipdedup
.RI [ options ] " input.zip output.zip"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zipdedup
reads a single-disk ZIP archive, hashes original entries' decompressed
content using SHA-1, and converts later identical members to Method 92 RefPtr
records if and only if the archive size decreases. Existing compressed streams
are not recompressed. It writes to a distinct new archive, never mutating input.
The first physical copy is retained as the canonical source.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --dry-run
Analyze and report expected savings; do not create an output archive.
.TP
.BR -v , " --verbose"
Show which member is retained for each duplicate.
.TP
.BR -q , " --quiet"
Suppress ordinary summary output.
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Display command syntax.
.TP
.B --version
Display program version.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
Reference compressed data are the 20-byte SHA-1 digest of the uncompressed
content. Both source and reference use the WinZip 0x9903 central-directory
UUID/checksum extra field. Metadata, FWKCS MD5 fields, comments, and retained
compressed streams are preserved. Method 92 extraction needs a compatible
UnZip/WinZip implementation.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Encrypted, split, PKAV-authenticated, signed, or otherwise non-preservable
archives are rejected. Supported codecs depend on the linked UnZip build.
The current utility is a POSIX program written in strictly ANSI C89 syntax.
ZIP64 offsets exceeding the platform's C long file-position range are refused.
An existing output archive is never overwritten.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 for success (including dry run and no duplicate files); 1 for archive or
I/O errors; 2 for invalid arguments.
